A first typecheck run reported TS2339 "Property 'integration' does not exist
on type 'Manifest'" in branch.ts. That was my own harness: a symlinked
node_modules resolved @bisellium/adapter-native to the master checkout, not
the worktree. Clean once the workspace links point at the worktree. NOT a
defect in the PR; recorded so the claim isn't repeated.

Blocking
--------

B1. The stale-certificate promise does not fire for any opus that can reach
    the rebase. mergeOpusBranch refuses unless state === "done"
    (branch.ts:195). check.ts:462 scopes probatio.certifies.stale (and
    .dirty, and probatio.evidence.tree) to ACTIVE opera only —
    building/verifying/review — with an explicit comment that a done item's
    certificate is history. done is exactly and only the state merge accepts.
    So the note merge prints, "bisellium check will report
    probatio.certifies.stale", is false at the moment it prints.
    Probe: copied studio/ to a scratch dir, flipped W-026 to state: done
    (review passed), re-ran check --json.
      state: building -> 3 x probatio.certifies.stale on opera/W-026.md
      state: done     -> 0; W-026's findings are probatio.certifies (x2),
                        traditio.stage, process.review_tier. None about the
                        tree.
    Nothing *suppresses* the rule — the brief's phrasing — but the rule never
    applies to the population merge operates on. D-015's "Rebase forces
    re-verification, for free ... the evidence model already refuses stale
    certificates" does not hold as built: after a rebased merge the tree has
    changed, the certificates are stale, and check is silent. The PR's third
    Decisions bullet ("The rebase consequence is surfaced, never suppressed")
    is the load-bearing claim of the whole D-015 rebase story, and it is the
    one thing here that does not work. The note is also advisory-only even
    when it does fire (check.ts:464 level "advise"), which is a separate
    question for the architect.

B2. strategy: rebase + push: true + pr.required: true — the officina's own
    configured flow, studio/bisellium.yml — fails on any opus branch that is
    already on origin. The rebase rewrites the branch; pushRef
    (branch.ts:163-167) runs a plain `git push origin <branch>`, which the
    remote rejects non-fast-forward.
    Probe (lab/C): fresh repo + bare origin, opus branch pushed to origin
    first (i.e. a PR exists on it), trunk ahead, then merge:
      push to origin failed: ! [rejected] opus/W-900 -> opus/W-900
        (non-fast-forward)
      exit 1
      local opus/W-900:  0e79159 opus work / 7c2405a trunk moved / de6938c init
      origin opus/W-900: 10353ef opus work / de6938c init
    The rebase is not rolled back, so the run leaves local and remote
    diverged, exits 1, and the error blames the push without ever saying the
    branch was rewritten. This is not hypothetical for this repo: origin
    holds opus/W-026 and PR #2 is open against it, so running
    `bisellium merge W-026 --studio studio --repo .` today lands exactly
    here. A rebase-then-push path needs --force-with-lease (and a test that
    pushes the branch before merging, which behaviour 14 does not).

Advisory
--------

A2. "Every exit path carries a note" (PR body) is false. After a successful
    rebase three failure paths return with no note: ff-merge failure
    (branch.ts:263), trunk-fetch failure (:270), branch-delete failure
    (:284-288). The code comment at :238 says "on either exit path below",
    which is accurate for the two success paths; the PR body generalised it.
    The delete path is the one that matters — the trunk really advanced.
    Probe (lab/F): rebase strategy, builder worktree checked out on the opus
    branch, no pr.required:
      master fast-forwarded to opus/W-900, but could not delete opus/W-900:
        ... used by worktree at .../lab/F/wt — switch off it and delete manually
      exit 1;  master log: a614f67 opus work / 5616f3a trunk moved / 3049468 init
    Rebased commits on the trunk, exit 1, no word about stale certificates.

A3. pull_after_push is unreachable under the studio's own configuration.
    pullTrunk is called only on the landing path (branch.ts:294); with
    pr.required: true merge returns at :256 first. studio/bisellium.yml sets
    both pull_after_push: true and pr.required: true, so as shipped that
    setting can never run in this officina. It is still validated by check
    and documented in the manifest — a setting that reads as live and is not.

A4. The PR's "Known follow-up" describes a problem that does not exist. No
    process.* rule reads or assumes any integration strategy: the only rule
    ids in packages/cli/src/rules/process.ts are process.cascade,
    process.review_tier, process.tdd, process.checkpoint, and grepping
    check.ts + rules/*.ts for rebase / pull request / opus branch returns only
    this PR's own new lines. So nothing today is "wrong for an officina
    configured the other way", and D-015's "process.* rules must read the
    setting" is vacuously satisfied. The scoping call is right; the stated
    reason is wrong. The feature is coherent as shipped — it is merge's own
    behaviour, and nothing enforces the flow either way.

A5. An invalid strategy: value silently degrades to fast_forward
    (branch.ts:63), and an unparseable or missing manifest does the same
    (:57-61). check blocks the first, but merge does not run check. A typo
    ("rebse") gets a silent fast-forward refusal instead of "unknown
    strategy". Documented as deliberate ("defensive, not a second
    validator"), and defensible — but a setting the operator explicitly wrote
    and misspelled deserves an error rather than a downgrade.

A6. Behaviour 7's test asserts where the worktree forked FROM and nothing
    about where the work goes TO — and its own cleanup block deletes
    `bisellium/${sella}/1` (run.test.ts, finally block), so the suite names
    the branch the work actually lands on while asserting past it. That is
    the break found by hand today (docs/SESSION-HANDOFF.md): run --opus forks
    from opus/<id> but commits to bisellium/<sella>/<n>, which merge never
    reads. One more assertion — opus/W-100 contains the commit after the run
    — would have caught it in both earlier rounds. Root cause is the brief's
    behaviour-7 wording, not the builder; it belongs in the follow-up opus,
    not here.

A7. integration: is a manifest contract addition and is undocumented in
    docs/ADOPTION.md, which is where wip_limit (line 173) and source_excludes
    (line 176) are documented and which line 42 presents as the manifest key
    list. The only prose is a TS interface comment and the two manifests'
    own comments. D-015's principle is "configuration the user can see".

A8. The brief's "Files owned" (branch.ts, branch.test.ts) is stale: this PR
    also changes check.ts, check.test.ts, adapters/native/src/index.ts and
    two manifests. D-015 authorises the work and it cannot be done inside
    branch.ts; amend the brief rather than exceed the ownership line quietly.

The merge_commit refusal — upheld
---------------------------------
Correct call, and correctly built. Probe (lab/E): strategy: merge_commit ->
`integration.strategy "merge_commit" is declared but not implemented — use
"fast_forward" or "rebase"`, exit 1, trunk untouched, branch untouched. It
refuses before computing merge-base, so no side effect precedes the refusal.
Mutation M6 below shows the suite catches a silent downgrade. A merge-commit
policy nobody has exercised would be guessed message format and guessed
conflict handling, and D-015's kill_when contemplates deleting strategies
rather than accumulating them. An enumerated-and-refused value is honest
configuration; a silent downgrade to fast_forward would not be.

The four owed reds — genuine
----------------------------
studio/ci/reds/W-026/{01,02,05,07}.log. All four are assertion-level failures
(the FAIL lines are check() assertions with surrounding PASS lines proving the
module loaded and the suite ran to completion), and no two cover the same
behaviour: 01 createOpusBranch must not move HEAD; 02 duplicate-branch
refusal; 05 conflict wording vs divergence wording; 07 run --opus fork source.

The earlier builder's claim that these could not be produced was wrong, and I
verified that rather than taking the logs on trust. Reproduced two from my own
mutations of the shipped code:
  01: `git branch <b>` -> `git checkout -b <b>`
      FAIL  stays on original branch                opus/W-026
  05: conflict probe short-circuited to never fire
      FAIL  error names the conflict, not divergence
      FAIL  names the conflict against master, not sidebranch
Both match the recorded logs line for line. Behaviours 10-16 are ordinary
pre-implementation reds, also assertion-level, also distinct (10 manifest
validation, 11 rebase-then-ff, 12 rebase conflict, 13 push, 14 pr.required,
15 merge_commit, 16 trunk name).

Mutation — the suite bites
--------------------------
Six mutations of branch.ts, each reverted, `branch.test.ts .` re-run:
  M1  trunk advance `git fetch . <b>:<trunk>` -> `git merge --ff-only <b>`
      (round 2's finding 5, the original defect)              2 FAIL
  M2  strategy setting ignored, always fast_forward           9 FAIL
  M3  staleNote suppressed                                    1 FAIL
  M4  pr.required ignored                                     4 FAIL
  M5  push ignored                                            1 FAIL
  M6  merge_commit silently downgraded to fast_forward        4 FAIL
M1 is the one asked for: the merge target is pinned by assertion, not merely
observed. The suite does not repeat round 2's failure mode.

Configuration behaviour, driven end to end through the CLI
----------------------------------------------------------
Each case: throwaway repo, bare local origin, a full copy of
studio/bisellium.yml with the integration block varied, opus at state: done.

  A   no integration: block, diverged branch
      -> "branch opus/W-900 has diverged from master — rebase first", exit 1,
         origin untouched, branch intact. Identical to pre-PR behaviour.
  A2  no integration: block, fast-forwardable
      -> "merged opus/W-900 into master", exit 0, branch deleted, origin NOT
         pushed (origin still at the old tip). Identical to pre-PR behaviour.
  B   rebase + push + pull_after_push + pr.required (the real studio config)
      -> exit 0; local master untouched; opus branch rebased onto the trunk;
         origin has both refs; both notes printed (PR-required note and the
         stale-certificate note); nothing landed.
  C   as B, branch pre-pushed to origin  -> B2 above.
  D   rebase + push + pull_after_push, no pr.required
      -> "merged opus/W-900 into master" + stale note, exit 0; local and
         origin master identical; branch deleted.
  E   merge_commit -> refused, trunk untouched (above).
  F   rebase, branch checked out in another worktree -> A2 above.
  H   acceptance criterion, two opera: W-901 and W-902 each on their own
      branch, merged in sequence under strategy: rebase
      -> both exit 0, master = W-902 work / W-901 work / init, both files
         present, no branches left. Round-2 finding 8 is now met: this is
         the substantive thing the PR buys, and it works.

Verdict
-------
FAIL, round 3. Two blocking findings, both narrow.

B1 is the reason. The rebase consequence is the mechanism D-015 leans on to
answer "should merge re-run gates", and as built the mechanism is silent for
every opus merge will accept. Either the rule has to cover the state merge
operates on, or the note has to stop citing a rule that will not fire and the
enforcement has to live somewhere that does. That is the architect's call,
not a builder's guess.

B2 is a one-flag fix plus the test that would have caught it.

Everything else is advisory and none of it needs to hold the PR. The reds are
real and were re-derived, not trusted; the suite bites on the merge target;
the configuration does what the manifest says on every path except the two
above; the default with no integration: block is byte-for-byte the old
behaviour; and the acceptance criterion this opus has failed twice is now
demonstrably met.

